10) Aggregate Functions
Definition
Aggregate Functions perform a calculation on multiple rows and return a single value.
They are mainly used to calculate totals, averages, highest values, lowest values, and counts.

Types of Aggregate Functions
COUNT()
SUM()
AVG()
MIN()
MAX()

1. COUNT()
Definition
COUNT() is used to count the number of rows.

Syntax
SELECT COUNT(column_name)
FROM table_name;
Example
SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM Employees;

Returns the total number of employees.

COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column)
COUNT(*) → Counts all rows, including rows containing NULL values.
COUNT(column) → Counts only non-NULL values in that column.

2. SUM()
Definition
SUM() is used to calculate the total of a numeric column.

Syntax
SELECT SUM(column_name)
FROM table_name;

Example
SELECT SUM(salary)
FROM Employees;

Returns the total salary of all employees.

3. AVG()
Definition
AVG() is used to calculate the average value of a numeric column.

Syntax
SELECT AVG(column_name)
FROM table_name;

Example
SELECT AVG(salary)
FROM Employees;

Round to 2 decimal places:

SELECT ROUND(AVG(salary),2)
FROM Employees;

4. MIN()
Definition

MIN() returns the smallest value from a column.

Example
SELECT MIN(salary)
FROM Employees;

Returns the lowest salary.

5. MAX()
Definition
MAX() returns the largest value from a column.

Example
SELECT MAX(salary)
FROM Employees;

Returns the highest salary.
Aggregate Functions with WHERE
We can filter rows before performing the calculation.

Example

SELECT AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
WHERE department = 'IT';

Important Points
Aggregate functions return one value.
COUNT(*) counts all rows.
COUNT(column) ignores NULL.
SUM(), AVG(), MIN(), and MAX() ignore NULL values.
Aggregate functions are often used with GROUP BY.

          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

11) GROUP BY
Definition
GROUP BY is used to group rows that have the same value in one or more columns. It is mainly used with aggregate functions.

Syntax
SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name;
Example

Count employees in each department.

SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department;

Find total salary of each department.

SELECT department, SUM(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department;

Find average salary of each city.

SELECT city, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY city;
GROUP BY with WHERE

WHERE filters rows before grouping.

SELECT department, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
WHERE salary > 50000
GROUP BY department;

GROUP BY with ORDER BY
SELECT department, COUNT(*) AS total_emp
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department
ORDER BY total_emp DESC;

Important Points
GROUP BY creates groups based on one or more columns.
It is mostly used with aggregate functions.
WHERE executes before GROUP BY.
Every selected column must either:
appear in GROUP BY, or
be inside an aggregate function.

            -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12) HAVING
Definition
HAVING is used to filter grouped data after the GROUP BY clause.

Difference Between WHERE and HAVING

             WHERE	                                                                            HAVING
Filters rows	                                                                        Filters groups
Executes before GROUP BY                                                               	Executes after GROUP BY
Cannot use aggregate functions	                                                        Can use aggregate functions
Can be used without GROUP BY	                                                        Usually used with GROUP BY

Syntax
SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name
HAVING condition;
Example

Departments having more than 5 employees.

SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department
HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;

Cities whose average salary is greater than 50000.

SELECT city, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY city
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000;

WHERE + HAVING Together
SELECT department, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
WHERE salary > 40000
GROUP BY department
HAVING AVG(salary) > 60000;

SQL Execution Order
FROM
↓
WHERE
↓
GROUP BY
↓
HAVING
↓
SELECT
↓
ORDER BY

Important Points
WHERE filters rows.
HAVING filters groups.
HAVING is mostly used with aggregate functions.
WHERE and HAVING can be used together.
For better compatibility across different databases, write:
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000

instead of
HAVING avg_salary > 50000
because not all databases allow aliases inside HAVING.

